                0.341
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.11: Depiction of molecules of water"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.338,
            0.197,
            0.537,
            0.218
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.3.2 Compounds"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.336,
            0.22,
            0.882,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": "Why cannot we separate hydrogen and oxygen present in water by physical means?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.336,
            0.261,
            0.884,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": "In water, the particles of hydrogen and oxygen are so tightly attached to each other that it is generally impossible to separate them apart using physical methods. That is why water is a compound. **Compounds** are formed when different elements combine in fixed"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
            0.363,
            0.237,
            0.504
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.365,
            0.46,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "Oh! This is really fascinating—hydrogen is a fuel, oxygen supports combustion, whereas w<u>ater extinguishes fire.</u>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.473,
            0.353,
            0.884,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "ratios to form something entirely new. The properties of compounds are different from those of elements forming that compound. The constituent elements of a compound cannot be separated by any physical method. From Activity 8.3, we"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.463,
            0.883,
            0.538
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "find that molecules of water are made of two different elements: hydrogen and oxygen (Fig. 8.11), combined chemically in a fixed ratio. The ratio of the number of atoms of hydrogen to oxygen in water has been found to be 2:1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.541,
            0.883,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": "Are common salt and sugar elements or compounds? Let us find out."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.579,
            0.884,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": "Sodium, a soft metal, and chlorine, a hazardous gas, combine to form a harmless yet taste-enhancing substance that is essential for our lives. This substance is known as sodium chloride, which is made up of particles of sodium and chlorine in a 1:1 ratio. We learnt that dissolved sodium chloride (common salt) may be separated from water by the physical process of evaporation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.692,
            0.883,
            0.731
          ],
          "content": "Is it possible to separate sodium chloride into its elements by physical processes?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.284,
            0.732,
            0.877,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": "Let us now explore if we can separate the elements in sugar!"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.774,
            0.677,
            0.799
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 8.4: Let us experiment"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.063,
            0.817,
            0.163,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.825,
            0.296,
            0.846
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Safety first"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.475,
            0.813,
            0.882,
            0.834
          ],
          "content": "Put a teaspoon of sugar in a boiling tube."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.857,
            0.449,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": "This activity must be performed in the presence of a teacher."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.476,
            0.837,
            0.855,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "z Heat it gently as shown in Fig. 8.12a."
        },
